# Flink Keyed State and Aggregation Patterns

## Overview

This skill covers techniques for maintaining state per key in Apache Flink, including finding maximum values, aggregating across multiple windows, and combining results from different streams.

## Finding Maximum Value Across Windows

### Problem: Find the longest session (most events) per job

When you have multiple session windows per key and need to find the maximum:

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.java.tuple.Tuple2;

// Step 1: Count events in each session
DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Long>> sessionCounts = submitEvents
    .keyBy(event -> event.getJobId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .apply(new WindowFunction<TaskEvent, Tuple2<Long, Long>, Long, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void apply(Long jobId, TimeWindow window, Iterable<TaskEvent> events,
                          Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) {
            long count = 0;
            for (TaskEvent e : events) {
                count++;
            }
            out.collect(new Tuple2<>(jobId, count));
        }
    });

// Step 2: Find maximum count per job using reduce
DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Long>> maxPerJob = sessionCounts
    .keyBy(t -> t.f0)  // key by jobId
    .reduce((a, b) -> a.f1 > b.f1 ? a : b);  // keep the larger count
```

## KeyedProcessFunction for State Management

### Tracking Maximum with ValueState

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueState;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueStateDescriptor;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.KeyedProcessFunction;
import org.apache.flink.util.Collector;

public class MaxTracker extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Tuple2<Long, Long>, Tuple2<Long, Long>> {
    private transient ValueState<Long> maxCount;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        ValueStateDescriptor<Long> descriptor =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("maxCount", Long.class);
        maxCount = getRuntimeContext().getState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Tuple2<Long, Long> value, Context ctx, Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out)
            throws Exception {
        Long currentMax = maxCount.value();
        if (currentMax == null || value.f1 > currentMax) {
            maxCount.update(value.f1);
        }
    }
}
```

## Outputting Results When Job Finishes

### Using CoProcessFunction to Join Task and Job Events

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.co.CoProcessFunction;

public class JoinWithJobEvents extends CoProcessFunction<
        Tuple2<Long, Long>,  // Session counts (jobId, count)
        JobEvent,            // Job events
        Tuple2<Long, Long>>  // Output when job finishes
{
    private ValueState<Long> maxSessionCount;
    private ValueState<Boolean> jobFinished;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        maxSessionCount = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("maxCount", Long.class));
        jobFinished = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("finished", Boolean.class));
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement1(Tuple2<Long, Long> sessionCount, Context ctx,
                                Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) throws Exception {
        // Update max if this session is larger
        Long currentMax = maxSessionCount.value();
        if (currentMax == null || sessionCount.f1 > currentMax) {
            maxSessionCount.update(sessionCount.f1);
        }

        // If job already finished, emit immediately
        Boolean finished = jobFinished.value();
        if (finished != null && finished) {
            out.collect(new Tuple2<>(ctx.getCurrentKey(), maxSessionCount.value()));
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement2(JobEvent jobEvent, Context ctx,
                                Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) throws Exception {
        // Check for job finish (event type 4 = FINISH, 3 = FAIL, 5 = KILL)
        if (jobEvent.getEventType() == 4 || jobEvent.getEventType() == 3 ||
            jobEvent.getEventType() == 5) {
            jobFinished.update(true);
            Long max = maxSessionCount.value();
            if (max != null) {
                out.collect(new Tuple2<>(jobEvent.getJobId(), max));
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Global Window with Custom Trigger

For aggregating all session results per job:

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.GlobalWindows;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.triggers.CountTrigger;

sessionCounts
    .keyBy(t -> t.f0)
    .window(GlobalWindows.create())
    .trigger(CountTrigger.of(1))  // Fire for each element
    .reduce((a, b) -> a.f1 > b.f1 ? a : b);
```

## Using AggregateFunction

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.functions.AggregateFunction;

public class CountAggregator implements AggregateFunction<TaskEvent, Long, Long> {
    @Override
    public Long createAccumulator() {
        return 0L;
    }

    @Override
    public Long add(TaskEvent value, Long accumulator) {
        return accumulator + 1;
    }

    @Override
    public Long getResult(Long accumulator) {
        return accumulator;
    }

    @Override
    public Long merge(Long a, Long b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

// Usage with session window
sessionCounts = submitEvents
    .keyBy(TaskEvent::getJobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(
        new CountAggregator(),
        new ProcessWindowFunction<Long, Tuple2<Long, Long>, Long, TimeWindow>() {
            @Override
            public void process(Long jobId, Context context, Iterable<Long> counts,
                              Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) {
                for (Long count : counts) {
                    out.collect(new Tuple2<>(jobId, count));
                }
            }
        }
    );
```

## Reduce for Finding Maximum

```java
// Simple reduce to find max
DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Long>> maxPerJob = sessionCounts
    .keyBy(t -> t.f0)
    .reduce(new ReduceFunction<Tuple2<Long, Long>>() {
        @Override
        public Tuple2<Long, Long> reduce(Tuple2<Long, Long> a, Tuple2<Long, Long> b) {
            return a.f1 > b.f1 ? a : b;
        }
    });
```

## Pattern: Emit Result at End of Stream

Use ProcessFunction with timers or custom triggers:

```java
public class EmitOnComplete extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Tuple2<Long, Long>, Tuple2<Long, Long>> {
    private ValueState<Long> maxCount;
    private ValueState<Long> lastUpdateTime;

    @Override
    public void processElement(Tuple2<Long, Long> value, Context ctx,
                              Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) throws Exception {
        Long current = maxCount.value();
        if (current == null || value.f1 > current) {
            maxCount.update(value.f1);
        }
        lastUpdateTime.update(ctx.timestamp());

        // Register timer to emit result after watermark passes
        ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(ctx.timestamp() + 1);
    }

    @Override
    public void onTimer(long timestamp, OnTimerContext ctx,
                       Collector<Tuple2<Long, Long>> out) throws Exception {
        // Emit current max
        Long max = maxCount.value();
        if (max != null) {
            out.collect(new Tuple2<>(ctx.getCurrentKey(), max));
        }
    }
}
```

## Tips

1. **reduce() is incremental**: Each new element immediately updates the accumulated result
2. **State is per-key**: ValueState maintains separate values for each key automatically
3. **Timers**: Use event-time timers when you need to trigger actions based on watermarks
4. **Output timing**: Consider when you want results emitted - per window, per update, or at job completion

